        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343345",
          "text": "<p>What is the location of parent node for any arbitrary node $i$ in an array implementation of a complete binary tree in C language?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>floor$(i /2)$</li>\n\t<li>floor$(i+1) /2$</li>\n\t<li>floor$((i-1)/2)$</li>\n\t<li>ceil$((i-1)/2)$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,binary-tree,c-language",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343306",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following two structure declarations and their initialization in C language.</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nstruct node1\n{\n    int a1, a2;\n}mynode1[10000];\n\nfor(i = 0; i &lt; 10000; i++)\n{\n    mynode1[i].a1 = rand();\n    mynode1[i].a2 = rand();\n}\nstruct node2\n{\n    int a1[10000], a2[10000];\n}mynode2;\n\nfor(i = 0; i &lt; 10000; i++)\n{\n    mynode2.a1[i] = rand();\n    mynode2.a2[i] = rand();\n}</pre>\n\n<p><br>\nAssume size of integer as $4$ bytes, a $64$ byte cache line, and a $32 KB$ fully associative cache implementation with all lines invalidated initially. If $n_1$ and $n_2$ denote the number of cache misses during the initializations of $mynode1$ and $mynode2$ respectively and if both $mynode1$ and $mynode2$ are aligned to cache lines with no other intervening cache accesses, the absolute value of $(n_1 - n_2) = $ _____</p>",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "0",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,cache-memory,array",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343333",
          "text": "Two balanced binary search trees are given with $m$ and $n$ elements respectively. They can be merged into another balanced binary search tree in $\\Theta((m+n)^a {(\\log (m+n))}^b)$ time. $2.a + 3b =$ _____",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "2",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,tree",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343347",
          "text": "<p>If the elements “A”, “B”, “C” and “D” in order are to be placed in a queue and stack and are deleted one element at a time (operations to the stack and queue being independent and deletion can happen in between insertions), which of the following is not a possible order for their removals from the queue and stack respectively?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>ABCD, ABCD</li><li>DCBA, DCBA</li><li>ABCD, DCBA</li><li>All the above choices are possible</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,stack,queue",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343326",
          "text": "The maximum number of leaf nodes in a tree with degree $5$ and height (starting from $1)\\;4$ is __________",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "125",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,tree",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343343",
          "text": "Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding a doubly linked list?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> It is possible to implement a doubly linked list with a single pointer at each node by storing XOR of addresses of previous and next nodes.</li><li>  It is possible to implement a doubly linked list with a single pointer at each node by storing XOR of addresses of current node and next node</li><li> It is possible to implement a doubly linked list with a single pointer at each node by storing XOR of addresses of current node and previous node</li><li>We need minimum two pointers at each node for a doubly linked list implementation</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,linked-list",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343310",
          "text": "<p>The best data structures in terms of asymptotic time complexities for performing the following operations respectively are</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>CONCATENATION (A, B): Concatenate all elements of A and B together</li>\n\t<li>Insert(T, key): Insert key to T</li>\n\t<li>Delete (T, key): Delete key from T if present</li>\n\t<li>Search (T, key): Returns true if key is present in T</li>\n</ul>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>Stack, Binary Search Tree, Sorted Array, Linked List</li>\n\t<li>Linked List, Stack, Binary Search Tree, Sorted Array</li>\n\t<li>Linked List, Stack, Sorted Array, Binary Search Tree</li>\n\t<li>Stack, Linked List, Sorted Array,&nbsp;Binary Search Tree</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343322",
          "text": "If the best time complexity (not the best case input but the best algorithm which will work for any input) for finding the shortest path between two keys $a$ and $b$ in a balanced binary search tree of $n$ nodes is denoted by $\\Theta(n^a (\\log n)^b),$ then $2^a\\times 3^b =$_____",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "3",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,binary-search-tree",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343341",
          "text": "<p>Let $L$ be a linked list of $n$ elements and $I$ be another linked list of $k$ node positions in $L$ ($n/4 &lt; k &lt; n/2)$ and elements in $I$ are not necessarily in sorted order. The best possible algorithm (use $O(1)$ extra space if required and assuming only comparison based sorting) to print the values in $L$ as per the node positions in $I$ will run in _____</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\Theta(n)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\Theta (n \\log n)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\Theta (n .k \\log k) $</li>\n\t<li>$\\Theta (nk)$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,linked-list",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343324",
          "text": "<p>What is the best time complexity (not the best case input but the best algorithm which will work for any input) for finding the lowest common ancestor for two keys $a$ and $b$ in a binary search tree of $n$ nodes and height $h$?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\Theta(n)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\Theta(n \\log n)$</li>\n\t<li>$O(h)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\Omega(n)$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,binary-search-tree",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343339",
          "text": "<p>The time complexity of the below code is</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nint isPalindrome ( char * string, int n )\n{\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;\n    if (string[0] != string[n-1]) return 0;\n    return isPalindrome(string + 1, n-2);\n}</pre>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\Theta(n)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\Theta (n \\log n)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\Theta(\\log n)$</li>\n\t<li>$\\Theta(n^2)$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,time-complexity",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343308",
          "text": "<p>Consider a STACK being implemented using two QUEUES by favoring the PUSH operations to POP. If $k$ PUSH operations and $l$ POP operations are done on this STACK with $n$ elements initially $(k,l &lt;&lt; n)$, the minimum sum of the corresponding ENQUEUE and DEQUEUE operations will be</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$O(kl)$</li>\n\t<li>$O(ln)$</li>\n\t<li>$O(k+l)$</li>\n\t<li>$O(k)$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,stack",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343320",
          "text": "In a binary search tree the following key values (not necessarily in the order given) are encountered while searching for the key $29.$\n$$3,7,13,19, 37,43,51,59.$$ The total number of possible orders in which the given keys of the binary search tree could have been visited is _____",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "70",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,binary-search-tree",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343315",
          "text": "Let $f(n),g(n)$ and $h(n)$ be the time complexities of three programs and let $f(n) = o(g(n))$ and $h(n) = \\omega(g(n)).$ Which of the following is VALID?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $f(n) = \\Theta(h(n))$</li><li>  $g(n) = O(f(n))$</li><li> $h(n) = \\Omega(g(n))$</li><li>$g(n) = \\Omega(h(n))$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,time-complexity,asymptotic-notations",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343350",
          "text": "Which of the following is not a valid sequence of key traversals while searching the key $44$ in a binary search tree?<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $11,88,22,77,33,66,44$</li><li>  $11,22,33,44$</li><li> $88,11,22,77,55,44$</li><li>$66,11,22,77,33,44$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,queue",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343335",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following C function to traverse a linked list.</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nvoid traverse(struct node *root)\n{\n    while (root)\n    {\n        printf(\"%d \", root-&gt;data);\n        root = root-&gt;next;\n    }\n}</pre>\n\n<p><br>\nWhich of the following is $\\textbf{TRUE}$ about above function?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>The function may crash when the passed linked list is empty</li>\n\t<li>The function does not print the last node when the linked list is not empty</li>\n\t<li>The function is implemented incorrectly because it changes the beginning of&nbsp;the linked list</li>\n\t<li>The function is implemented correctly and print all the values of the linked list starting from root</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,linked-list,c-language",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343357",
          "text": "<p>The cut vertices in the given graph are</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"216\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"189\"></p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>E and F</li>\n\t<li>A, C and D</li>\n\t<li>C, E and F</li>\n\t<li>B and C</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,graph-theory,graph-connectivity",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343348",
          "text": "For a given simple graph $G$ having $v$ vertices and $e$ edges which is connected and has no cycles, which of the following statements is true?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $v=e$</li><li>  $v = e+1$</li><li> $v + 1 = e$</li><li>$v = e-1$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,graph-theory",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343317",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following representation of a ternary tree where $A: B,C,D$ denote that $B,C$ and $D$ are the child nodes of $A.$</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$1:2,3,4$</li>\n\t<li>$2:5,6$</li>\n\t<li>$3:7$</li>\n\t<li>$4:8,9,10$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>If the root of the above tree is passed to the following C function (assume the the child nodes are represented by child1, child2 and child3 and all NULL values are assigned properly), the return value will be _____</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nint foo(struct node *root)\n{\n    if(!root) return 0;\n    return 1 + foo(root-&gt;child1) +\n        foo(root-&gt;child2) +\n        foo(root-&gt;child3);\n}</pre>",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "10",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,tree",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343337",
          "text": "<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nvoid fun(struct node* start)\n{\n    if(start == NULL)\n        return;\n    if(start-&gt;next != NULL )\n        fun(start-&gt;next-&gt;next);\n    printf(\"%d \", start-&gt;data);\n}</pre>\n\n<p>The sum of the values printed by the above function when passed with the head to the following linked list is _____<br>\n$$1\\to2\\to 3\\to 4\\to 5\\to 6\\to 7$$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "16",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,linked-list",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343353",
          "text": "<p>The data structures most suitable to do an inorder and level order traversals of a binary tree respectively are</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>Stack and Queue</li>\n\t<li>Queue and Stack</li>\n\t<li>Stack and Stack</li>\n\t<li>Queue and Queue</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,stack,queue",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343319",
          "text": "<p>What will be the output of the C program?</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\n#include&lt;stdio.h&gt;\nint main()\n{\n    int a[][4] = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};\n    int (*ptr)[4] = a;\n    printf(\"%d %d \", (*ptr)[0], (*ptr)[1]);\n    ++ptr;\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", (*ptr)[0], (*ptr)[1]);\n    return 0;\n}\n</pre>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$0 \\ 1 \\ 3 \\ 4$</li>\n\t<li>$0 \\ 1 \\ 4 \\ 5$</li>\n\t<li>$0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \\ 3$</li>\n\t<li>$0 \\ 1 \\ 6 \\ 7$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,array,pointers",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343312",
          "text": "<p>Pick the correct order of asymptotic growth rate for the following functions.<br>\n$$n^{1/4}, e^n, 3^n, 1.001^n, n^{1000}, n^{\\sqrt n}, n!$$</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$n^{1/4} &lt;n^{\\sqrt n}&lt;1.001^n &lt;e^n&lt; 3^n&lt; &nbsp;n!&lt; n^{1000}$</li>\n\t<li>$n^{1/4}&lt; n^{1000}&lt;1.001^n &lt;e^n&lt; 3^n&lt;n^{\\sqrt n}&lt; &nbsp;n!$</li>\n\t<li>$1.001^n &lt; n^{1/4}&lt;n^{\\sqrt n}&lt;1.001^n &lt;e^n&lt; 3^n&lt; n^{1000}&lt; n!$</li>\n\t<li>$n^{1/4}&lt; n^{1000}&lt;n^{\\sqrt n}&lt;1.001^n &lt;e^n&lt; 3^n&lt; &nbsp;n!$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,asymptotic-notations",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343328",
          "text": "Let $f(n),g(n)$ and $h(n)$ be the time complexities of three programs. Let $f(n) = O(g(n))$ and $h(n) = O(g(n)).$ Which of the following is necessarily FALSE?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $f(n) = O(h(n))$</li><li>  $g(n) = O(f(n))$</li><li> $h(n) = \\Omega(g(n))$</li><li>None of the above are necessarily FALSE</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,time-complexity,asymptotic-notations",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343314",
          "text": "Consider an array with the following elements <br />$$1\\;3\\;2\\;7\\;6\\;10\\;5\\;4.$$<br />The minimum number of interchanges required to make it a min-heap is _____",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "1",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,binary-heap",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343330",
          "text": "Given an array of elements $5,7,9,1,3,10,8,4,$ which of the following is not a possible array implementation of min-heap of these elements? <br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $1,3,4,7,5,8,9,10$</li><li>  $1,4,3,8,9,5,7,10$</li><li> $1,3,4,5,8,7,9,10$</li><li>All are min-heaps</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,binary-heaps",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343352",
          "text": "The prefix form of $A/B- (C * D * E)$ is?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $-/* * ABCDE$</li><li>  $-/ABCD**DE$</li><li> $-/AB**CDE$</li><li>$-A/BC*DE*$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,infix-prefix,stack",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343354",
          "text": "What is the value of the postfix expression $8 \\ 7 \\ 2 \\ 4 \\ + \\ – \\ *?$:",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "8",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,numerical-answers,stack,infix-prefix",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343332",
          "text": "<p>The below code is for adding a new element to an array implementation of a min-heap. parent(i) returns the index of the parent node in the heap and size stores the number of elements in the heap.</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nvoid swap(int *a, int *b)\n{\n    int temp = *a;\n    *a = *b;\n    *b = temp;\n}\nvoid insert(int elem)\n{\n     size++;\n    int i = size - 1;\n    heap[i] = elem;\n    while (i &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; heap[parent(i)] &gt; heap[i])\n    {\n     [FILL ME]\n      i = parent(i);\n    }\n}</pre>\n\n<p><br>\nThe correct replacement for [FILL ME] will be</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>\n\t<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nswap(&amp;heap[i], &amp;heap[parent(i)]);</pre>\n\t</li>\n\t<li>\n\t<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nswap(&amp;heap[i], &amp;heap[i+1]);</pre>\n\t</li>\n\t<li>\n\t<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nswap(&amp;heap[i], &amp;heap[2*i]);</pre>\n\t</li>\n\t<li>\n\t<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nswap(&amp;heap[2*i], &amp;heap[parent(i)]);</pre>\n\t</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "go2026-ds-1,binary-heaps",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "343355",
